WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
The city of Tokyo is built
around and over rivers.

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:06.800
When you travel around here, it is striking
just how often you cross water.

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:12.500
And from up above, you can see this
whole area is flat, low-lying coastal land.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:15.040
Some parts of Tokyo even sit below sea level.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:18.110
They've sunk because of decades of
pumping out groundwater.

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:21.039
The two and a half million people
in those low-lying areas

00:00:21.039 --> 00:00:24.550
are protected by a network of
flood gates and aging levees,

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:27.859
which'll be fine unless sea levels rise.

00:00:27.859 --> 00:00:29.029
But even inland,

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:33.199
a huge part of this megacity is built
in river basins and flood plains.

00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:35.420
And as the climate breaks down,

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:38.969
rain storms here are getting more intense
and more frequent.

00:00:38.969 --> 00:00:40.749
If you want to prevent river flooding,

00:00:40.749 --> 00:00:44.050
or drain neighbourhoods
that are starting to flood:

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:45.370
where do you put the water?

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:47.049
If you pump it out of that neighbourhood,

00:00:47.049 --> 00:00:49.639
you're likely just moving the flood next door.

00:00:49.639 --> 00:00:51.429
The best plan would, in theory,

00:00:51.429 --> 00:00:54.440
be to pump the floodwater
all the way to the ocean,

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:57.000
or at least to a tidal river
that can deal with it.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:02.519
But for that, you would need tunnels and
buffer tanks on an almost unimaginable scale.

00:01:02.519 --> 00:01:06.470
Now, despite what some
breathless blog posts might claim,

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:08.649
the whole metropolis doesn’t have that.

00:01:08.649 --> 00:01:13.860
But there is one place, about an hour
north of the centre of Tokyo, that does.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:24.439
This is the Metropolitan Area
Outer Underground Discharge Channel,

00:01:24.439 --> 00:01:27.409
or at least, one part of it: the holding tank.

00:01:27.409 --> 00:01:31.220
It is almost impossible to convey the scale
of this on camera.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:32.880
[IN JAPANESE:]

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.820
If the Otoshifurutone and Naka rivers
start to flood,

00:01:54.829 --> 00:01:55.999
this is where the water will drain to.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.080
Actually, where the water
is sort-of still draining to.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:01.800
There was a massive rainstorm about two days ago,

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.500
and there is still water in the system.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:07.140
I am not allowed down on the floor,
just in case.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.060
[IN JAPANESE:]

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:48.900
This tank, and the kilometres of tunnels and
all the silos that bring water to it,

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:50.760
cost nearly a quarter of a trillion yen,

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:54.240
that's about two billion US dollars,
at the end of the 20th century.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.220
That was when the Japanese government was
spending money

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:59.750
to finance enormous
infrastructure projects like this,

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:01.250
to try and stimulate the economy.

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:04.340
Which, for long-term security, is great:

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:07.189
but it took a national recession
to make this happen.

00:03:07.189 --> 00:03:10.849
A plan like this only works if a government
wants to spend a colossal amount of money,

00:03:10.849 --> 00:03:12.230
and in the 21st century,

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:15.600
there aren't many governments
willing to do that.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:17.740
Thank you to all the team here
for letting me down in the tank,

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:20.300
and up on these catwalks.
You can find out more about them

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:21.980
and their work
at the link in the description.

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:27.000
Echo!

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:30.180
[echoes continue and fade]

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:32.900
That goes for a long time.

